module flmd-criminal-local-rules.local-rules

// The Middle District's criminal-case rules state no period. Unless
// otherwise provided by law, the United States begins a criminal action
// in a division in which at least one defendant committed a charged
// offense, and the judge must transfer it to the division to which it is
// most directly connected or in which it is most conveniently advanced
// (Local Rule 1.04(c)); the clerk classifies each initial paper as civil,
// criminal or miscellaneous and assigns it at random (Local Rule 1.05(a)).
// Before judgment in, or dismissal of, a criminal action, a lawyer or law
// enforcement agent must not extrajudicially and publicly disclose
// information if the disclosure will interfere with a fair trial or
// otherwise prejudice the administration of justice (Local Rule 3.11(a)),
// with the examples of Local Rule 3.11(b). A judge can temporarily modify
// or suspend any rule except Local Rule 1.05(a) (Local Rule 1.01(b)).
// Every criminal period (presentment, the preliminary hearing, Rule 12
// motions, Rule 16 discovery, Rules 29, 33 and 35, the presentence report,
// the Speedy Trial Act and FRAP 4(b)) is lit/frcrimp's. The earlier
// outline's FRCrimP flow and its decisions belong to lit/frcrimp and are
// removed here: no reporter text is vendored.
